(()=>{
const cards=[["What is a porcelain-margin metal–ceramic restoration?", "It is a metal–ceramic restoration in which porcelain rather than externally visible metal forms the selected marginal area, often on the facial and interproximal aspects."], ["What are other names for a porcelain-margin restoration?", "The book lists terms including porcelain-butt margin, porcelain-shoulder margin and collarless metal–ceramic restoration."], ["Why can a visible metal collar be an esthetic concern?", "The dark or metallic appearance may be noticeable near the gingival margin, especially in visible anterior areas. Its appearance can also be influenced by preparation, design and later tissue recession."], ["Does a porcelain margin guarantee healthy gingiva?", "No. The restoration’s fit and contour, periodontal condition, hygiene, clinical care and other case factors remain important."], ["What three domains does the book identify in planning a successful case?", "Treatment planning, clinical execution and dental laboratory outcomes. Each affects the final result."], ["Why is a clear finish line important?", "A legible and accurately reproduced finish line helps the technician assess where to form and evaluate the porcelain margin."], ["Why does the preparation need sufficient space?", "Space is needed for the prescribed metal, opaque and shoulder/body porcelains without creating excessive contours or compromising the intended material thickness."], ["How does the metal framework relate to the shoulder?", "The book illustrates a framework terminating in relation to the prepared shoulder, leaving room for the porcelain margin. Alternative cutbacks exist and must suit the selected system and case."], ["What is the role of shoulder porcelain?", "In the illustrated technique it forms the ceramic margin and acts as a scaffold or foundation to be veneered with body porcelain."], ["Why check the intaglio surface before seating?", "Debris or raised defects may prevent complete seating or damage the gypsum die; inspect and correct them using the approved procedure."], ["How does the book classify porcelain-margin methods?", "It divides them into an indirect refractory-die method and direct methods including platinum foil and four direct-lift variants."], ["What is the indirect refractory-die method?", "The margin is produced on a duplicate heat-resistant die rather than directly on the master gypsum die. Fit on the master die is evaluated after the refractory material is removed."], ["What is a limitation of the refractory-die method?", "The duplicate die can be fragile, and the fit on the original master die cannot be checked until the refractory die has been removed. Repetition may be needed if the result is unsatisfactory."], ["What is a direct method?", "In the book’s classification a direct method forms the margin on the master die. Four direct-lift variants involve removing the unfired margin from that die for firing."], ["Why is platinum foil described as a hybrid?", "The book lists it among direct methods but explains that it has characteristics of both direct and indirect techniques because the foil supports the margin during firing."], ["Name the four direct-lift variants listed by Naylor.", "Direct lift with conventional dentin porcelain, porcelain–wax, porcelain–resin and shoulder porcelain."], ["What historical concerns are described for porcelain–wax and porcelain–resin?", "The book cites research reporting reductions in density and apparent tensile strength, and possible optical changes, when wax or resin is incorporated in the porcelain mix."], ["Why use a compatible shoulder porcelain in the illustrated direct-lift technique?", "The commercial shoulder porcelain is designed for the marginal area and compatible processing with the specified metal–ceramic system. Follow the current product instructions."], ["Does shoulder porcelain undergo no shrinkage?", "No. It still shrinks during sintering; the chapter discusses assessing and correcting the margin after firing."], ["What is the conceptual sequence for a direct-lift porcelain margin?", "Prepare and inspect the die and opaqued coping, use a compatible separator, form and condense the margin, lift it carefully, fire as specified and reassess fit; correct if needed."], ["Why is a separator used on the die?", "It helps prevent the porcelain from adhering to the die so the delicate unfired restoration can be lifted. Separator selection must be compatible with the material and current instructions."], ["Why should the margin be lifted carefully?", "The unfired shoulder porcelain is fragile and can be distorted or damaged during removal from the die."], ["What is checked before firing the margin?", "Inspect for unwanted porcelain or debris, including on the intaglio and beyond the intended finish line, while maintaining the shape and support required by the system."], ["Why is marginal fit checked after the first firing?", "The porcelain can shrink during firing and create a gap or irregularity. Re-evaluation shows where a correction may be necessary."], ["Why avoid excessive first-application bulk in the book’s example?", "Because shoulder porcelain shrinks toward bulk, excessive buildup can draw the margin farther from the finish line and increase the marginal opening."], ["Why might a second shoulder-porcelain firing be needed?", "An additional application can compensate for shrinkage or correct a deficiency found after the first firing. The actual number of firings is material- and case-specific."], ["What happens once an acceptable shoulder margin is obtained?", "In the illustrated sequence, the remaining dentin and enamel body buildup is completed using the appropriate material system and techniques."], ["Why should marginal-gap numbers from the book not be treated as universal?", "They come from different studies, materials, designs and measurement methods; a research result does not guarantee the fit of a particular restoration."], ["How is the finished porcelain margin evaluated?", "Assess seating and the marginal relationship, including visible gaps, overextensions and irregularities, using appropriate magnification and the lab’s quality procedure."], ["Should historical acid steps in the foil technique be used as current lab instructions?", "No. Historical methods are described for understanding. Follow current institutional safety procedures and the specific material instructions; hazardous acids require specialised controls and must not be improvised."], ["Are 360-degree porcelain margins fully taught as a step-by-step technique in this chapter?", "No. The chapter illustrates examples but states that the technical skills and procedures for a complete 360-degree porcelain margin exceed the scope of the book."], ["What case factors should be evaluated besides the margin itself?", "Consider contour, shade, translucency, occlusal and proximal relationships, periodontal condition, patient hygiene and the clinician’s treatment plan."]];const questions=[{"q": "What is a key esthetic aim of a porcelain-margin metal–ceramic restoration?", "o": ["Remove all metal from the restoration", "Reduce visible metal at the selected margin", "Avoid any need for a finish line", "Guarantee no future gingival recession"], "a": 1, "e": "The selected marginal area is made in porcelain to reduce visible metal."}, {"q": "Which is another name discussed for a porcelain margin?", "o": ["Porcelain-butt margin", "Wax sprue button", "Gypsum indexing notch", "Amalgam margin"], "a": 0, "e": "The book lists porcelain-butt margin and several related terms."}, {"q": "A successful porcelain margin depends on:", "o": ["Porcelain alone", "Only laboratory shade", "Only the cement brand", "Planning, clinical execution and laboratory work"], "a": 3, "e": "The book identifies all three domains as important."}, {"q": "Which technique is classified as indirect in the chapter?", "o": ["Direct-lift shoulder porcelain", "Porcelain–wax direct lift", "Refractory die", "Porcelain–resin direct lift"], "a": 2, "e": "The refractory-die technique uses a duplicate heat-resistant die."}, {"q": "What is a limitation of the refractory-die technique?", "o": ["The master die is never used in the case", "Fit on the master die is assessed after removing the refractory die", "It does not involve any porcelain", "It automatically gives a perfect margin"], "a": 1, "e": "The indirect margin is checked on the original master die after removal of the refractory material."}, {"q": "What makes platinum foil a hybrid in the book’s explanation?", "o": ["It has aspects of both direct and indirect methods", "It is not mentioned in the chapter", "It is a brand of shoulder powder", "It replaces the master die permanently"], "a": 0, "e": "The book lists it as direct while noting hybrid characteristics."}, {"q": "Which is a direct-lift variant listed in the textbook?", "o": ["Alginate impression only", "Enamel etching alone", "Gypsum pouring only", "Shoulder porcelain"], "a": 3, "e": "Shoulder porcelain is one of the four direct-lift variants."}, {"q": "What are the historical concerns with porcelain–wax and porcelain–resin?", "o": ["They guarantee zero voids", "They make the metal unnecessary", "They may reduce fired density and apparent tensile strength", "They remove the need to assess fit"], "a": 2, "e": "The book cites research reporting reductions in density and apparent tensile strength."}, {"q": "What must be considered when designing metal cutback?", "o": ["A single number fits every patient", "Support, material compatibility and the case prescription", "Only the colour of the die", "How quickly the furnace cools, with no other factor"], "a": 1, "e": "Design depends on the system, preparation, support and case."}, {"q": "What is a function of shoulder porcelain in the illustrated technique?", "o": ["Forming a margin that supports the body buildup", "Replacing the impression tray", "Creating a wax sprue", "Calibrating the furnace"], "a": 0, "e": "Shoulder porcelain forms the ceramic margin and provides a foundation for body porcelain."}, {"q": "What is the purpose of a compatible die separator?", "o": ["Make every margin thick", "Replace opaque porcelain", "Guarantee no firing shrinkage", "Help release the delicate unfired margin from the die"], "a": 3, "e": "A compatible separator facilitates removal without adhesion to the die."}, {"q": "Why must an unfired direct-lift margin be handled carefully?", "o": ["It has already reached its final glazed strength", "It is made entirely of metal", "It is fragile and can be distorted", "It cannot contact a brush"], "a": 2, "e": "The unfired porcelain margin can be damaged during removal."}, {"q": "How does shoulder porcelain behave during firing?", "o": ["It never changes dimension", "It can shrink towards bulk", "It becomes gypsum", "It automatically expands to fill every gap"], "a": 1, "e": "The book describes shrinkage toward bulk."}, {"q": "What may excessive first-application bulk contribute to?", "o": ["A larger marginal opening after shrinkage", "A guaranteed perfect seal", "Automatic elimination of all corrections", "A different impression material"], "a": 0, "e": "Excessive bulk can pull the margin farther from the finish line as it shrinks."}, {"q": "Why check the margin after the first firing?", "o": ["Because no further steps are possible", "To avoid ever inspecting the die", "To replace the framework", "To identify shrinkage-related gaps or irregularities"], "a": 3, "e": "Post-firing inspection shows whether correction is needed."}, {"q": "How many correction firings are universally required?", "o": ["Exactly one for every system", "Exactly three for every case", "There is no universal number", "None are ever possible"], "a": 2, "e": "The number of applications and firings depends on material and the result."}, {"q": "Which liquid should be used with shoulder porcelain?", "o": ["Any available investment liquid", "The liquid specified by the material manufacturer", "Any household acid", "Whatever was used for another unrelated product"], "a": 1, "e": "Use the prescribed compatible liquid and current instructions."}, {"q": "How should historical chemical steps in an older technique be treated?", "o": ["As historical information; follow current safety rules", "As mandatory instructions for every student", "As a replacement for the material IFU", "As proof that no PPE is needed"], "a": 0, "e": "Historical hazardous steps are not automatically safe or appropriate today."}, {"q": "Are study marginal-gap measurements universal guarantees?", "o": ["Yes, all techniques have the same result", "Yes, any study value defines every clinical case", "Yes, if the shade is correct", "No; material, design and measurement vary"], "a": 3, "e": "Study values should not be applied as a universal acceptance guarantee."}, {"q": "What does the book say about full 360-degree porcelain margins?", "o": ["They are the only type shown", "They require no specific skills", "Examples are shown, but advanced fabrication is outside the book’s scope", "They cannot exist"], "a": 2, "e": "The chapter includes examples but does not fully teach the advanced fabrication procedure."}];let current=0,revealed=false,answers=Array(questions.length).fill(null);const flash=document.getElementById('flashCard'),flashSide=document.getElementById('flashSide'),flashText=document.getElementById('flashText'),flashCount=document.getElementById('flashCount');function showFlash(){flashText.textContent=cards[current][revealed?1:0];flashSide.textContent=revealed?'Answer':'Question';flashCount.textContent=`Card ${current+1} of ${cards.length}`;flash.setAttribute('aria-label',revealed?'Show flashcard question':'Reveal flashcard answer')}flash.addEventListener('click',()=>{revealed=!revealed;showFlash()});document.getElementById('prevFlash').addEventListener('click',()=>{current=(current-1+cards.length)%cards.length;revealed=false;showFlash()});document.getElementById('nextFlash').addEventListener('click',()=>{current=(current+1)%cards.length;revealed=false;showFlash()});const quiz=document.getElementById('quizList');function renderQuiz(){quiz.innerHTML='';questions.forEach((q,i)=>{const box=document.createElement('article');box.className='qcard';const meta=document.createElement('div');meta.className='qmeta';meta.textContent=`Question ${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}`;const title=document.createElement('h3');title.textContent=q.q;const opts=document.createElement('div');opts.className='options';const feedback=document.createElement('p');feedback.className='feedback';feedback.setAttribute('aria-live','polite');feedback.hidden=answers[i]===null;const choice=answers[i];feedback.textContent=(choice===null?'Answer: ':choice===q.a?'Correct. ':'Not quite. ')+q.o[q.a]+'. '+q.e;q.o.forEach((text,j)=>{let b=document.createElement('button');b.type='button';b.className='opt';b.textContent=String.fromCharCode(65+j)+'. '+text;if(choice!==null){b.disabled=true;if(j===q.a)b.classList.add('correct');else if(j===choice)b.classList.add('incorrect')}b.addEventListener('click',()=>{answers[i]=j;renderQuiz();const updated=document.querySelectorAll('.qcard')[i].querySelector('.feedback');updated.setAttribute('tabindex','-1');updated.focus({preventScroll:true})});opts.appendChild(b)});box.append(meta,title,opts,feedback);quiz.appendChild(box)});const answered=answers.filter(x=>x!==null).length,correct=answers.filter((x,i)=>x===questions[i].a).length;document.getElementById('score').textContent=`${answered} of ${questions.length} answered · ${correct} correct`}document.getElementById('resetQuiz').addEventListener('click',()=>{answers=Array(questions.length).fill(null);renderQuiz()});showFlash();renderQuiz();const qaSearch=document.getElementById('qaSearch'),qaItems=[...document.querySelectorAll('.qa-item')];function filterQa(){const term=qaSearch.value.trim().toLowerCase();let visible=0;qaItems.forEach(item=>{const yes=item.textContent.toLowerCase().includes(term);item.hidden=!yes;if(yes)visible++});document.getElementById('qaCount').textContent=visible+' of '+qaItems.length+' questions';document.getElementById('noQa').hidden=visible!==0}qaSearch.addEventListener('input',filterQa);document.getElementById('qaExpand').addEventListener('click',()=>qaItems.filter(item=>!item.hidden).forEach(item=>item.open=true));document.getElementById('qaCollapse').addEventListener('click',()=>qaItems.filter(item=>!item.hidden).forEach(item=>item.open=false));let oldDetails=[];window.addEventListener('beforeprint',()=>{oldDetails=qaItems.map(x=>({open:x.open,hidden:x.hidden}));qaItems.forEach(x=>{x.hidden=false;x.open=true})});window.addEventListener('afterprint',()=>qaItems.forEach((x,i)=>{if(oldDetails[i]){x.hidden=oldDetails[i].hidden;x.open=oldDetails[i].open}}));

})();
